## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical control of network addressing across environments, Terraform input validation, and CI gating to prevent risk without applying changes.

## Key Concepts
- CIDR overlap validation in Terraform
- Module input validation
- CI gates using plan
- Multi-env network design

## Code Example
```terraform
variable "environment_cidrs" {
  type = map(string)
  validation {
    condition = alltrue([
      for env, cidr in var.environment_cidrs : cidr != "" && !contains(var.allocated_cidrs, cidr)
    ])
    error_message = "CIDR overlaps with allocated CIDRs or is empty"
  }
}
variable "allocated_cidrs" {
  type = set(string)
  description = "CIDRs already allocated to prod/staging"
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle dynamic CIDR allocation across environments?
- How would you handle updates to the allocated CIDR store without causing plan drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical use of for_each with maps, resource interdependencies, and stable identities across environments. Delivers hands-on pattern candidates expect in production.

## Key Concepts
- for_each with maps provides stable keys and predictable IDs
- referencing attributes from one resource in another (subnet_id in NAT GW)
- tagging and AZ assignment per subnet
- outputs to surface IDs for downstream resources
- when to prefer for_each vs count (stable keys vs positional indexing)

## Code Example
```javascript
variable "subnets" {
  type = map(object({ cidr_block = string; az = string }))
  default = {
    az1 = { cidr_block = "10.0.1.0/24", az = "us-east-1a" },
    az2 = { cidr_block = "10.0.2.0/24", az = "us-east-1b" },
    az3 = { cidr_block = "10.0.3.0/24", az = "us-east-1c" }
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "private" {
  for_each = var.subnets
  vpc_id            = var.vpc_id
  cidr_block        = each.value.cidr_block
  availability_zone = each.value.az
  tags = {
    Name = "private-${each.key}"
  }
}

output "private_subnet_ids" {
  value = [for s in aws_subnet.private : s.id]
}
```

```javascript
resource "aws_nat_gateway" "ngw" {
  for_each = aws_subnet.private
  allocation_id = aws_eip.ngw[each.key].id
  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.private[each.key].id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- When would you choose for_each over count in real projects?
- How would you migrate from count-based to for_each-based resources without breaking state?
- How would you validate AZ distribution and CIDR correctness after apply?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical gating of Terraform plans using a dedicated QA backend, plan export, and automated validation to prevent prod touches.

## Key Concepts
- Backend aliasing and per-env state isolation
- terraform plan output in JSON
- Scripting plan validation with jq/Node.js
- CI gating and manual approvals for apply

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to validate plan.json plan boundaries
const fs=require('fs');
const plan=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('plan.json','utf8'));
const resources=plan?.planned_values?.root_module?.resources||[];
const ok=resources.every(r=>r.address.startsWith('modules/qa/')||r.address.includes('environment=qa'));
process.exit(ok?0:1);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multi-module repos?
- How do you handle drift checks beyond plan validation?
